    <h2 id="how-to-use-heading">How to Use This Calculator — Step by Step</h2>
    <ol style="margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-5) var(--space-6);">
      <li style="font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: var(--leading-normal); margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
        <strong>Identify your gap period.</strong> Count the total number of full calendar months between the end of your Initial Enrollment Period (the month after you turned 65) and the month you first enrolled in a Medicare Part D plan or Medicare Advantage plan with drug coverage.
      </li>
      <li style="font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: var(--leading-normal); margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
        <strong>Enter the number of uncovered months.</strong> Enter the full months count in the calculator above. Do not count partial months — only full calendar months count toward the penalty.
      </li>
      <li style="font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: var(--leading-normal); margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
        <strong>Read your penalty amount.</strong> The calculator multiplies your months by 1% of the 2026 national base beneficiary premium ($36.78), rounds to the nearest $0.10, and displays your permanent monthly surcharge.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <div style="background: #fff8f0; border-left: 4px solid #E8761A; border-radius: 0 0.5rem 0.5rem 0; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin-top: 1.25rem;">
      <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 700; color: #E8761A; margin: 0 0 0.25rem 0;">💡 Pro Tip</p>
      <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">If you believe you had creditable drug coverage (employer plan, VA, TRICARE) that the IRS is not counting, request a creditable coverage letter from your former employer or VA and submit it with your Part D plan enrollment. This can eliminate or reduce the penalty.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       3. PLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="partd-section" aria-labelledby="explanation-heading">
    <h2 id="explanation-heading">What Your Part D Penalty Means — and Why It Matters</h2>

    <p>
      The Medicare Part D late enrollment penalty is a lifetime monthly surcharge added to your prescription drug coverage. If you go without creditable drug coverage for 63 or more consecutive days after turning 65 (or after your Initial Enrollment Period ends), CMS adds this penalty to your monthly bill for as long as you have Medicare drug coverage.
    </p>

    <h3>How the Part D Penalty Formula Works</h3>
    <p>
      Unlike Part B (which charges 10% per full 12-month period), Part D accumulates at <strong>1% per full month</strong>. CMS calculates the surcharge using three factors:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Uncovered months:</strong> The total number of full calendar months you were eligible for Part D but did not have creditable drug coverage.</li>
      <li><strong>The 1% rate:</strong> You pay 1% of the national base beneficiary premium for every uncovered month.</li>
      <li><strong>National Base Beneficiary Premium:</strong> A standard national figure published annually by CMS. In 2026, this figure is <strong>{BASE_PREMIUM_2026}</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      CMS calculates your raw penalty, then <strong>rounds it to the nearest $0.10</strong>. This rounded dollar amount is added to whatever monthly premium your chosen Part D plan or Medicare Advantage plan charges.
    </p>

    <div class="partd-info-box">
      <p class="partd-info-box-heading">Why the Part D penalty changes slightly each year:</p>
      <p>
        Because your penalty percentage (e.g., 14%) is multiplied by the CMS National Base Beneficiary Premium each year, your dollar surcharge will rise or fall slightly whenever CMS adjusts the national base rate. If the base premium increases from {BASE_PREMIUM_2026} in a future year, your monthly penalty increases proportionally.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       4. WORKED EXAMPLE (STEP BY STEP)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="partd-section partd-section--tinted" aria-labelledby="worked-example-heading">
    <h2 id="worked-example-heading">Step-by-Step Worked Example: Robert's Part D Penalty</h2>
    <p>
      Here is a step-by-step example showing how the <strong>part d late enrollment penalty calculator</strong> determines the exact monthly surcharge for a retiree.
    </p>

    <div class="partd-example-card">
      <div class="partd-example-scenario">
        <span class="partd-example-label">Scenario</span>
        <p>
          <strong>Robert</strong> turned 65 and his Initial Enrollment Period ended in <strong>June 2024</strong>. He took no daily prescription medications, so he decided to skip Part D to save money. In <strong>August 2025</strong>, during a routine checkup, his doctor prescribed two brand-name medications. Robert signed up for a Part D plan during the Fall Annual Enrollment Period, with coverage starting <strong>January 1, 2026</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="partd-example-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Count uncovered months:</strong>
          Robert had no creditable drug coverage from <strong>July 2024 through December 2025</strong> = <strong>18 full months</strong> without coverage (well beyond the 63-day grace period).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Calculate penalty percentage:</strong>
          18 months × 1% = <strong>18% penalty rate</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Apply 2026 National Base Premium:</strong>
          18% × {BASE_PREMIUM_2026} = <strong>$6.6204 per month</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Apply CMS rounding rule:</strong>
          CMS rounds $6.6204 to the nearest $0.10 = <strong>$6.60 per month</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Final monthly impact:</strong>
          Robert selects a basic Part D plan that costs $25.00/month. His actual monthly bill is $25.00 + $6.60 penalty = <strong>$31.60 per month</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lifetime cost:</strong>
          Robert pays an extra $79.20 per year. Over 20 years of retirement, his 18-month delay costs him <strong>$1,584.00 in extra surcharges</strong>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       COMMON MISTAKES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="partd-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Part D Late Enrollment Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Assuming COBRA Drug Coverage Is Creditable</h3>
    <p>COBRA continuation coverage is typically creditable for Part D, but some plans are not. If CMS determines your COBRA drug plan was not creditable, you will owe a penalty for every month you held it.</p>
    <p>The fix is ask your COBRA plan administrator specifically if the drug coverage meets CMS creditable coverage standards and get a written Notice of Creditable Coverage annually.</p>

    <h3>Not Documenting VA or FEHB Coverage Before Enrolling in Part D</h3>
    <p>VA prescription drug coverage IS creditable, but you must be able to prove continuous coverage if CMS questions your penalty calculation.</p>
    <p>The fix is before enrolling in Part D, gather a written record of your VA enrollment dates from your VA patient portal or VA clinic.</p>

    <h3>Forgetting That the Dollar Amount Changes Each Year</h3>
    <p>Because your Part D penalty percentage is fixed but multiplied by the CMS national base rate annually, the dollar amount you pay can rise or fall every January.</p>
    <p>The fix is check your plan's EOB each January to verify your penalty amount has been updated correctly by your plan.</p>
  </section>

